# Production database safety

The EC2 site (`65.1.92.180` / `65.1.92.180.sslip.io`) and its MariaDB data are **production**.

## Default stance

- Prefer **read-only** inspection (list users, check settings, read logs, curl health checks).
- Do **not** assume staging. If unsure whether a command writes, ask first.

## Never do without explicit user approval

- Password resets (`update_password`, forced new passwords)
- Creating, disabling, deleting, or renaming Users / Companies / DocTypes / data
- Bulk SQL `UPDATE` / `DELETE` / schema changes
- Clearing or rewriting Email Queue, Activity Log, or other live records
- `bench migrate`, `bench reinstall`, DB restore/drop, or destructive `docker compose` data wipes
- Changing Email Account credentials or other secrets unless the user asked

## Allowed without asking (when debugging live issues)

- Re-apply **already-known** hot-patches / branding scripts that restore app code (not user data)
- `bench --site frontend clear-cache` and container restarts that do not wipe volumes
- Read-only `bench console` / SQL `SELECT` style checks

## If a write is required

1. State exactly what will change and which rows/users are affected.
2. Wait for explicit approval (e.g. “reset password for X”, “fix email account”).
3. Prefer the smallest possible change; avoid cascading “cleanup” edits.
